A1. This clears the way for a lot of talk. ~ AHEM
A5. If you swallow this you'll regret it. ~ BAIT
A9. To smooth down an upheaval in the city. ~ PAVE
A13. This grass makes hay when the sun shines. ~ SERE
A14. Lindbergh's Morrow. ~ ANNE
A15. An idol word. ~ ICON
A16. This has replaced the old oaken bucket. ~ PAIL
A17. Two thousand lbs. and then some. ~ TONS
A18. What the coon saw when his sugar said a mouthful. ~ GOLD
A19. A lid of distinction. ~ STETSON
A21. What the easiest way does to you. ~ TEMPTS
A23. It wouldn't be odd if this had a V. ~ EEN
A24. Surrounded by wets this somehow remains dry ~ ISLE
A25. This is better to have than a heart. ~ SPADE
A28. You can live on this indefinitely (abbr.). ~ ST
A29. A high-priced bar. ~ INGOT
A33. An islet. ~ AIT
A34. Roman bill collector. ~ PATER
A37. Useful to travel in and costs very little. ~ TUBE
A38. What a dry party generally is. ~ FLOP
A40. What Nathan does to most shows besides reviewing them. ~ DAMNS
A42. The earth revolves around this according to Pa. ~ SON
A43. Chic Sales's favorite inventor. ~ ELMER
A45. This is never past but always present. ~ IS
A46. The old man's crowning glory (pl.). ~ PATES
A48. Better than a loss except if you're reducing. ~ GAIN
A50. What Walter Raleigh was besides chivalrous. ~ SIR
A51. The kind of boy that's always on the go. ~ ERRAND
A54. What you get into when you're too hot. ~ TANTRUM
A58. These are apt to be overdue. ~ DUES
A59. This lords it over a Viscount. ~ EARL
A61. This hardly means a thing. ~ IOTA
A62. The man who paid dearly for his porridge. ~ ESAU
A63. The way of all garbage. ~ ASEA
A64. Misunderstood children. ~ STEP
A65. This shows it belongs to Ned. ~ NEDS
A66. What the amateur farmer sows but seldom reaps. ~ SEED
A67. A hardy heroine. ~ TESS

D1. These are more deadly than parlor snakes. ~ ASPS
D2. Something found in the tropics. ~ HEAT
D3. A big pond. ~ ERIE
D4. What Cleopatra did when things got too hot. ~ MELTED
D5. You get money for waving this. ~ BATON
D6. It won't be long now. ~ ANON
D7. A good place to go when you're out. ~ INN
D8. Take these before flying. ~ TESTS
D9. The cause of a black eye. ~ PIGMENT
D10. This is apt to annoy you while speeding (two words). ~ ACOP
D11. This will jolt the most hardened criminal. ~ VOLT
D12. We think it would be nice if these two met. ~ ENDS
D20. What a secret is apt to do. ~ SEEP
D22. This man was a high priest and rated B. C. ~ ELI
D24. This is news (pl.). ~ ITEMS
D25. What the yegg blew as a child. ~ SAFE
D26. You won't be arrested if you take this. ~ PILL
D27. A very small matter. ~ ATOM
D28. Once in, this doesn't want to come out. ~ STAIN
D30. Nothing but a blow hard. ~ GUST
D31. If you play hard this will take your wind. ~ OBOE
D32. The kind of spots that are hard to get off a Scotchman (pl.). ~ TENS
D35. This is sure to get an answer. ~ AD
D36. The King's sea dogs (abbr.). ~ RN
D39. Did you ever see a horse fly? This one did. ~ PEGASUS
D41. What you do to a roulette wheel. ~ SPIN
D44. What Coolidge did before he did not choose to run. ~ RAN
D47. A Greenwich Village product. ~ ARTIST
D49. If you have any of these, now is the time to use them. ~ IDEAS
D50. A green not on the links. ~ SALAD
D51. Where your mail will be forwarded hereafter. ~ EDEN
D52. Don't let this deceive you. ~ RUSE
D53. A good thing to do with your morning paper. ~ READ
D54. A sun shade. ~ TREE
D55. How Polly learned to talk. ~ ROTE
D56. Tribe of reds (pl.). ~ UTES
D57. This keeps people from going wrong (pl.). ~ MAPS
D60. Peer Gynt's mammy. ~ ASE


